WEBVTT

00:07.200 --> 00:08.160
Tommy?

00:18.120 --> 00:19.320
[Tommy] Morning, Mrs. Ross.

00:22.120 --> 00:23.440
Have a seat.

00:29.720 --> 00:31.680
Thank you for seeing me, Mr. Shelby.

00:32.280 --> 00:33.800
I know how busy you are.

00:35.080 --> 00:36.280
How can I help you?

00:38.320 --> 00:41.160
Well, you see tomorrow
would have been my son's...

00:41.680 --> 00:43.040
Twenty-first birthday.

00:43.800 --> 00:45.040
Had he lived.

00:46.640 --> 00:48.760
And I'm having a bit of a thing. Just...

00:49.400 --> 00:52.640
Sandwiches and beer
and looking at old photos.

00:54.520 --> 00:56.520
And as a gesture,

00:58.120 --> 00:59.760
I thought I would invite your brother.

01:00.560 --> 01:01.520
Arthur.

01:05.680 --> 01:07.760
[Tommy] A gesture of what Mrs. Ross?

01:12.520 --> 01:13.840
Not exactly forgiving us.

01:16.080 --> 01:17.280
But something like that.

01:19.200 --> 01:20.960
And also it's a way of saying thank you

01:21.720 --> 01:23.160
to the Shelby family for helping

01:23.880 --> 01:25.400
me and my children get by

01:26.160 --> 01:27.960
since the death of my Edward.

01:29.840 --> 01:31.480
[Tommy] That's very kind of you.

01:33.400 --> 01:35.280
That's very kind. I will let Arthur know.

01:37.360 --> 01:38.800
It will be at my house at midday.

01:40.200 --> 01:41.360
He should come at midday.

01:43.000 --> 01:44.840
Your house midday tomorrow, all right?

01:45.840 --> 01:47.200
One of us will be there.

01:49.600 --> 01:50.520
It ought to be Arthur.

01:57.840 --> 01:59.160
Goodbye, Mrs. Ross.

02:01.320 --> 02:03.600
[footsteps receding]

02:05.840 --> 02:08.080
["Snake Oil" playing]

02:18.960 --> 02:20.400
♪ Yay ♪

02:22.120 --> 02:24.720
♪ You keep it slow ♪

02:25.800 --> 02:27.560
♪ Yay-yay ♪

02:29.120 --> 02:32.720
♪ You keep it low ♪

02:33.240 --> 02:36.560
♪ You cast a spell that keeps me wired ♪

02:36.680 --> 02:39.560
♪ Keeps me red, keeps me on fire ♪

02:39.800 --> 02:43.200
♪ So why do you take me down
Down to nothing ♪

02:43.360 --> 02:45.880
♪ Always out cold
Never been higher ♪

02:46.480 --> 02:47.920
♪ Oh ♪

02:49.640 --> 02:50.680
[Tommy] All right.

02:51.320 --> 02:52.480
At midday today,

02:52.840 --> 02:55.520
Arthur will be here. 47 Artillery Square.

02:56.600 --> 02:59.280
Like a goat, tied up for
the fucking tiger, eh, Arthur?

02:59.360 --> 03:00.280
Yeah, right.

03:02.880 --> 03:04.080
Dear Lord, forgive me.

03:05.240 --> 03:06.560
But vengeance is forthcoming...

03:08.160 --> 03:09.120
And long overdue.

03:12.640 --> 03:13.600
[Tommy] Right.

03:13.720 --> 03:15.840
There are two ways into Artillery Square.

03:16.240 --> 03:17.320
One here, on the towpath.

03:17.880 --> 03:19.160
and here, Navigation Street.

03:19.280 --> 03:20.360
Finn, take a rifle.

03:22.320 --> 03:24.360
You'll be here, Finn.
Covering the towpath.

03:24.680 --> 03:27.000
Most likely they will
come down Navigation Street.

03:27.720 --> 03:31.280
Johnny and Isiah will set up here
as hawkers selling salted pork.

03:31.520 --> 03:34.160
When you get the signal from me,
they can open fire.

03:34.240 --> 03:35.200
From you, right?

03:35.280 --> 03:37.760
Yeah, I'll be here
in the window overlooking.

03:37.880 --> 03:40.320
You still know what end
the bullets come out of, Tom?

03:40.640 --> 03:42.680
-[all chuckle]
-He knows.

03:43.560 --> 03:46.200
These men are coming for my brother.

03:47.560 --> 03:49.000
We're leaving him to be unarmed

03:49.880 --> 03:50.840
and unprotected.

03:52.280 --> 03:53.960
So this is my fucking business.

03:55.200 --> 03:56.400
Arthur, let's go.

03:57.960 --> 03:59.080
[Arthur] Look alive, soldier.

04:03.320 --> 04:04.200
[door opens]

04:07.760 --> 04:09.320
Just put them on the table.

04:17.080 --> 04:19.360
[dog barking in distance]

04:24.760 --> 04:26.560
[indistinct chatter]

04:29.680 --> 04:31.040
[lighter clicks]

04:42.680 --> 04:43.560
[vendor] Buy yourselves...

04:43.840 --> 04:48.280
Big and raw, ribs, cheek and tongue...

04:49.240 --> 04:51.720
Ribs, tongue, and cheek! Ah!

04:52.280 --> 04:54.280
[vendor continues chattering]

05:02.680 --> 05:04.280
[knock on door]

05:15.160 --> 05:16.280
Mr. Shelby.

05:20.280 --> 05:21.280
I'm glad you could come.

05:22.160 --> 05:23.320
[Arthur] Er...

05:24.840 --> 05:26.040
Thank you for the invitation.

05:27.800 --> 05:28.640
So...

05:30.720 --> 05:32.480
I don't allow firearms in the house.

05:35.240 --> 05:39.160
It's a good thing I'm not carrying
a firearm then ain't it, Mrs. Ross?

05:40.080 --> 05:41.320
Do you mind if I check?

05:44.320 --> 05:45.240
No.

05:58.200 --> 05:59.160
Come in.

06:10.280 --> 06:11.280
Take a seat.

06:13.240 --> 06:16.440
Would you like some tea
while we wait, Mr. Shelby?

06:17.800 --> 06:19.080
What are we waiting for?

06:21.360 --> 06:22.560
The other guests.

06:25.320 --> 06:26.920
[teapot clattering]

06:31.560 --> 06:33.880
[vendor speaking indistinctly]

06:43.000 --> 06:43.840
[whistles]

06:47.160 --> 06:48.560
[guns clicking]

07:00.120 --> 07:01.160
[car door shuts]

07:42.040 --> 07:43.760
-[opens faucet]
-[water flowing]

07:47.400 --> 07:49.080
So where are these other guests?

07:50.200 --> 07:51.120
[closes faucet]

07:51.200 --> 07:52.640
They will be here very soon.

07:58.480 --> 08:01.160
But in the meantime,
there are some things I want to say.

08:06.640 --> 08:07.880
Before they get here.

08:11.120 --> 08:12.080
By all means.

08:15.560 --> 08:17.600
I've heard stories that you have...

08:18.480 --> 08:20.920
Welcomed Jesus into your life?

08:21.200 --> 08:22.080
I have indeed.

08:23.840 --> 08:25.160
At the beseeching of your wife,

08:26.560 --> 08:28.040
who they say is a good woman.

08:30.200 --> 08:31.720
She is my shining light.

08:32.480 --> 08:34.240
Though her mother by all accounts was not.

08:38.000 --> 08:41.720
Do you think your repentance
will mean you are forgiven of your sins?

08:45.120 --> 08:46.080
We both know that...

08:47.360 --> 08:48.200
Forgiveness...

08:49.120 --> 08:50.320
Is out of my hands.

08:52.800 --> 08:54.960
I mean for murdering my son.

08:56.280 --> 08:57.840
That was a sporting accident.

09:01.760 --> 09:04.640
Because I have not yet found it
in my heart to forgive you.

09:07.840 --> 09:09.040
I don't think I ever will.

09:11.960 --> 09:12.840
Then...

09:13.840 --> 09:16.320
Why have you invited me here today?

09:18.600 --> 09:19.640
Hmm?

09:24.960 --> 09:26.120
[Mrs. Ross] Off you go now.

09:28.000 --> 09:30.360
Why am I here, Mrs. Ross?

09:34.480 --> 09:36.680
Why did you invite me here today?

09:58.760 --> 10:00.120
[man 1] Too late to back out now.

10:00.760 --> 10:01.760
[man 2] Here you go, mate.

10:02.000 --> 10:03.240
Last day of freedom, mate.

10:04.000 --> 10:05.600
[man 3] He scrubs up well, doesn't he?

10:05.760 --> 10:07.160
[man laughs]

10:09.680 --> 10:11.360
[suspenseful music playing]

10:22.200 --> 10:23.400
[Tommy] Now, Johnny, fire!

10:23.600 --> 10:24.600
Move!

10:27.960 --> 10:29.280
[gunshots]

10:30.840 --> 10:32.440
It's a fucking decoy!

10:32.560 --> 10:33.880
Bring the car around, now!

10:44.800 --> 10:45.880
What are you doing?

10:47.000 --> 10:48.400
[both grunting]

10:48.720 --> 10:49.840
[gunshot]

10:50.880 --> 10:52.200
Oh, shit.

10:57.120 --> 10:58.280
Arthur!

10:59.080 --> 11:00.160
Arthur,

11:00.600 --> 11:02.600
they're not here for you.
It must be Michael.

11:04.200 --> 11:06.160
-You fucking bitch!
-[clattering]

11:07.560 --> 11:08.840
You set me up, eh?

11:09.080 --> 11:11.080
You set me up...

11:11.880 --> 11:12.760
Or no?

11:13.360 --> 11:14.480
[whimpers]

11:14.560 --> 11:16.560
Arthur, you need to
get to the hospital now.

11:16.800 --> 11:18.160
-[grunts]
-[whimpers loudly]

11:19.560 --> 11:20.600
[growls]

11:23.280 --> 11:25.400
If your faith is real...

11:25.960 --> 11:27.760
I've got all the kids.

11:37.440 --> 11:38.600
[glass shatters]

11:42.400 --> 11:43.680
If you're here in one hour,

11:44.880 --> 11:45.920
I swear to God...

11:48.640 --> 11:53.560
I will cover you in tar
and fucking feathers.

11:58.680 --> 11:59.720
C'mon, Finn, let's go.

12:00.040 --> 12:01.360
[crying]

12:02.000 --> 12:03.080
[grunting]

12:10.000 --> 12:11.640
[door shuts and locks]

12:17.120 --> 12:18.720
Uh, uh, uh.

12:20.400 --> 12:21.280
Where I'm from,

12:22.240 --> 12:23.360
a hat on the bed...

12:24.240 --> 12:25.160
Unlucky.

12:29.200 --> 12:30.520
My family...

12:32.560 --> 12:34.120
...say it brings death.

12:36.920 --> 12:37.880
Maybe...

12:39.280 --> 12:40.440
Maybe that's what happened.

12:41.920 --> 12:44.840
Last time my men
were sent for you, you got lucky.

12:52.560 --> 12:53.600
Now...

12:58.880 --> 13:01.000
Your luck's run out.

13:04.080 --> 13:05.080
[softly] It's over.

13:07.600 --> 13:08.840
[breathing heavily]

13:09.760 --> 13:10.760
[gun clicks]

13:21.160 --> 13:22.400
[gun clicking]

13:33.720 --> 13:35.000
And tell your mother...

13:37.640 --> 13:38.600
We have a deal.

13:39.840 --> 13:41.520
[footsteps receding]

13:51.360 --> 13:52.440
[cocks gun]

13:54.640 --> 13:55.560
Come here.

13:58.640 --> 14:01.160
Michael? What happened, eh? What happened?

14:01.360 --> 14:03.200
They heard you, they heard you coming.

14:03.400 --> 14:04.840
The gun misfired.

14:05.160 --> 14:06.360
They ran away.

14:06.640 --> 14:07.560
Johnny!

14:10.080 --> 14:11.160
You came just in time.

14:12.240 --> 14:13.520
You all right? You all right?

14:14.600 --> 14:16.560
I'm fine. Fine.

14:17.200 --> 14:18.800
[Michael breathing heavily]

14:27.800 --> 14:28.880
[door opens]

14:28.960 --> 14:29.800
[Tommy] Oi!

14:30.200 --> 14:31.200
Get out.

14:31.680 --> 14:33.400
[indistinct chatter]

14:37.320 --> 14:39.000
Small Heath, 3-3-3.

14:40.840 --> 14:41.680
Charlie?

14:42.440 --> 14:44.920
Listen, Molly Gold
will be calling in four minutes.

14:45.320 --> 14:47.240
Tell them the Italians are around south.

14:48.440 --> 14:50.360
A Rolls Royce. Maybe two. Black.

14:51.280 --> 14:53.200
Tell them to expect
a full complement of men.

14:53.720 --> 14:55.120
We inflicted no casualties.

14:55.360 --> 14:57.320
I said we inflicted no fucking casualties.

15:03.040 --> 15:04.480
[exhales]

15:19.160 --> 15:20.280
-[Luca speaking Italian]
-[driver speaking Italian]

15:20.360 --> 15:22.960
There seems to be an
obstruction, Mr. Changretta...

15:37.280 --> 15:38.360
There's been an accident.

15:40.560 --> 15:42.280
Well, why don't you
move it out of the way?

15:42.480 --> 15:43.360
[policeman] What?

15:46.160 --> 15:48.960
I said why don't you clear it
so that we can get past?

15:49.640 --> 15:50.600
What's that accent?

15:52.280 --> 15:53.600
We're Americans.

15:53.840 --> 15:55.480
We're here on business.

15:56.200 --> 15:57.480
Well, you're gonna have to wait.

15:58.280 --> 15:59.360
Jesus Christ.

15:59.840 --> 16:02.760
We waited for you
fucking Yanks until 1918.

16:04.680 --> 16:06.280
It will be clear in 20 minutes, all right?

16:06.400 --> 16:10.320
Yeah, all right.
Thank you, Officer. Big fucking help.

16:12.360 --> 16:13.560
[speaking Italian]

16:13.640 --> 16:16.320
1918, what the fuck is he talking about?

16:18.360 --> 16:19.760
[in English] Talking about the war.

16:21.760 --> 16:23.520
Everything here is about the war.

16:27.400 --> 16:28.560
[sighs]

16:37.040 --> 16:39.960
C'mon, let's go move it ourselves.
Fucking idiots.

16:50.680 --> 16:52.760
What's this? Whose wagon is that?

16:53.240 --> 16:54.400
[policeman] They're gypsies.

16:55.280 --> 16:56.720
Tribe of fucking gypsies.

16:59.160 --> 17:00.400
We can move it, all right?

17:02.560 --> 17:04.160
I said it will be clear in 20 minutes.

17:06.160 --> 17:07.120
Let's go.

17:09.400 --> 17:10.920
We'll find another way out of here.

17:16.880 --> 17:19.800
Go. Get us the fuck out of here.

17:20.680 --> 17:21.680
[driver grunting]

17:21.760 --> 17:22.880
Come on, fucking move.

17:25.560 --> 17:27.000
Frankie, fucking move.

17:36.400 --> 17:37.360
[gunfire]

17:42.720 --> 17:43.600
Ahhh!

17:48.880 --> 17:49.800
[car engine starts]

17:53.400 --> 17:56.200
Put the guns down! Put the guns down!

17:58.680 --> 17:59.640
[groans]

18:10.240 --> 18:11.480
[Michael] He spared me.

18:15.560 --> 18:17.560
He said, "Tell your mum
that we have a deal."

18:21.720 --> 18:22.880
What deal?

18:24.800 --> 18:27.080
It will just be me and you
going to Australia, Michael.

18:28.120 --> 18:29.920
-Think about that.
-What deal, Mum?

18:31.400 --> 18:33.600
What deal did you make
with Luca Changretta?

18:43.600 --> 18:44.920
A deal to spare your life.

18:48.840 --> 18:50.000
In return for what?

18:56.520 --> 18:59.560
Men don't have the strategic intelligence
to conduct a war between families.

19:02.240 --> 19:04.840
Men are less good at
keeping secrets out of their lives.

19:07.640 --> 19:09.120
You agreed to give up Tommy?

19:10.760 --> 19:11.840
To save my life?

19:14.640 --> 19:16.080
You never forgave him, did you?

19:20.640 --> 19:22.160
We're out of our league.

19:25.640 --> 19:26.600
No.

19:28.840 --> 19:30.720
-We don't do that to our own.
-Think.

19:31.440 --> 19:34.280
If it wasn't for me you'd be smoke
blowing out of a mortuary chimney.

19:35.600 --> 19:36.680
Think about that.

20:01.120 --> 20:02.600
Uh, two.

20:03.520 --> 20:04.920
We killed two.

20:09.040 --> 20:10.200
You also shot a copper.

20:10.320 --> 20:12.560
Shot him in the arm with purpose.

20:13.240 --> 20:14.440
There's a copper on the floor,

20:14.520 --> 20:16.920
the Stratford Constabulary
will swarm all over it.

20:19.000 --> 20:20.320
The copper's alive.

20:20.720 --> 20:22.400
Got a description of the Italians.

20:23.080 --> 20:24.920
The Italians will be forced underground.

20:25.360 --> 20:26.720
We're gypsies.

20:27.440 --> 20:28.840
We're already underground.

20:35.520 --> 20:38.400
You yourself inflicted
no casualties this end so...

20:41.000 --> 20:42.080
You know,

20:42.400 --> 20:43.600
Mr. Shelby,

20:44.600 --> 20:47.360
maybe you've been pushing
pieces of paper around too long.

20:49.280 --> 20:50.720
Luca Changretta got away.

20:52.040 --> 20:53.080
You let him get away.

20:53.880 --> 20:57.600
When our paths cross
I won't be so careless.

20:59.880 --> 21:01.320
And when will your paths cross?

21:02.840 --> 21:03.840
Soon.

21:11.120 --> 21:13.080
[mellow guitar music playing]

21:43.920 --> 21:45.640
If her majesty smells like paraffin,

21:45.720 --> 21:48.200
it's because my old lamp
is smoking for want of fuel.

21:49.520 --> 21:51.280
Times are hard
in case you haven't noticed.

21:51.440 --> 21:53.480
I hope your insistence that I take

21:53.680 --> 21:55.640
a boat rather than a cab from the station

21:55.720 --> 21:57.280
wasn't purely for your own amusement.

21:57.360 --> 21:59.560
No. For the avoidance of death and kidnap.

22:00.520 --> 22:02.680
By boat, you get to see
the nicer parts of the city.

22:03.320 --> 22:06.040
He got us here, now.
You'll be all right. This way.

22:09.360 --> 22:10.960
Good morning, Lady Carleton.

22:11.440 --> 22:12.760
I'm not a lady.

22:13.040 --> 22:14.320
[door opens]

22:14.720 --> 22:15.840
Too right you're not.

22:18.360 --> 22:21.880
[Tommy] Shoeing, stabling, feed,
two hours a day in the gallops.

22:23.400 --> 22:24.680
Did you think of a name?

22:25.400 --> 22:27.200
Yes. Dangerous.

22:29.520 --> 22:30.480
"Dangerous", eh?

22:30.720 --> 22:35.040
Oh, a horse called Dangerous won the
Epsom Derby in 1833, so I stole the name.

22:35.440 --> 22:36.560
-I thought you'd approve.
-[lighter clicks]

22:39.440 --> 22:40.320
You should visit her.

22:40.840 --> 22:42.640
If I leave Small Heath, I'll be shot dead.

22:44.040 --> 22:45.240
Anyway, I'm busy.

22:45.640 --> 22:47.040
Well, the good news is

22:47.120 --> 22:49.440
your horse is the
fastest filly that I have ever ridden.

22:51.320 --> 22:53.120
These are her registration papers.

22:53.880 --> 22:55.080
Ready for your signature.

23:02.600 --> 23:03.920
So, is this why you came here?

23:05.720 --> 23:06.880
All this way in person?

23:09.440 --> 23:10.680
For my fucking signature?

23:15.680 --> 23:17.560
You've lost your wife...

23:19.560 --> 23:20.760
And now your brother, too.

23:23.240 --> 23:24.840
I thought it would make you different.

23:27.640 --> 23:29.120
But it doesn't seem to change you.

23:30.000 --> 23:31.480
Nothing seems to change you.

23:33.200 --> 23:34.320
[door opens]

23:34.520 --> 23:38.440
The end of year accounts
for the Gray Shelby Institute, Mr. Shelby.

23:38.960 --> 23:40.160
I beg your pardon, madam.

23:40.960 --> 23:42.400
Already signed off on those, Lizzie.

23:44.240 --> 23:45.680
What do you think of his shirt?

23:46.520 --> 23:48.800
He's having them made in London.
I'm not sure about them.

23:49.160 --> 23:50.440
All right, Lizzie, thank you.

23:50.720 --> 23:53.960
But, you know sometimes,
he sees something glamorous and expensive,

23:54.360 --> 23:55.720
he just can't resist it.

23:56.120 --> 23:57.200
He's so weak.

23:59.240 --> 24:00.360
Actually,

24:00.800 --> 24:02.920
speaking of the Gray Shelby Foundation,

24:03.160 --> 24:04.440
I'd like to make a donation.

24:05.960 --> 24:07.000
[clears throat]

24:08.160 --> 24:09.440
I'll leave it blank.

24:10.480 --> 24:12.240
You can decide how much, Mr. Shelby.

24:16.240 --> 24:17.320
Then perhaps,

24:18.240 --> 24:19.120
Lizzie?

24:20.160 --> 24:22.640
You can pop to the bank
and pay it in for me?

24:27.840 --> 24:29.280
That be all, Lizzie, thank you.

24:39.400 --> 24:40.400
Drink?

24:42.040 --> 24:42.880
No, thank you.

24:47.360 --> 24:48.680
Did you pack an overnight bag?

24:51.760 --> 24:52.960
No.

24:54.840 --> 24:55.800
Pity.

24:56.600 --> 24:57.640
Why?

24:58.200 --> 24:59.760
Well, because 25 minutes ago,

25:00.560 --> 25:02.000
the Birmingham branch

25:02.320 --> 25:03.600
of the train drivers union

25:03.760 --> 25:05.240
called a wildcat strike

25:05.640 --> 25:07.160
in support of the coal miners.

25:08.640 --> 25:10.040
No trains to London until tomorrow.

25:11.120 --> 25:12.640
It wasn't in any of the papers.

25:13.240 --> 25:14.840
It's why they call it a wildcat.

25:15.160 --> 25:16.360
No one knows.

25:16.600 --> 25:17.960
Apart from you, of course.

25:18.600 --> 25:19.800
Your city.

25:21.880 --> 25:23.840
So where would I stay in your city?

25:24.920 --> 25:26.720
I'll be free at four.
I could meet you then.

25:27.680 --> 25:28.840
Meet and do what?

25:32.880 --> 25:34.160
I'll pour you some gin...

25:35.800 --> 25:36.960
And I'll make myself...

25:38.240 --> 25:39.520
From my father's recipe.

25:40.600 --> 25:44.320
"Distilled for the eradication
of seemingly incurable sadness".

25:45.440 --> 25:46.880
I'm going to have that on the label.

25:47.360 --> 25:48.480
I'll book myself a room.

25:49.480 --> 25:51.680
Already done. The Midland. Suite.

25:52.760 --> 25:54.120
Stay there myself sometimes.

25:54.240 --> 25:55.560
Sometimes it's where you stay.

26:01.480 --> 26:03.120
But not tonight it would seem.

26:06.640 --> 26:09.160
Well, since I'm stuck here
maybe I should try your gin.

26:12.040 --> 26:12.960
4 o'clock then.

26:13.640 --> 26:14.640
Where?

26:19.320 --> 26:20.240
I'll find you.

26:27.760 --> 26:29.880
What the fuck was that about, Lizzie? Eh?

26:30.520 --> 26:31.640
Have a guess.

26:32.200 --> 26:33.760
You know, I can't even guess anymore.

26:33.840 --> 26:35.000
I'll be back at 4:00.

26:36.200 --> 26:37.560
Why is she still here?

26:37.920 --> 26:40.040
Something to do with
the coal miners, apparently.

26:41.640 --> 26:42.800
[door opens]

26:42.920 --> 26:44.560
[footsteps receding]

26:46.120 --> 26:49.480
Oh, today's your lucky day, sir.
I'll give you 3 to 1 on Raging Robbers.

26:50.040 --> 26:52.280
Come on, gents. Get your bets on.

26:52.440 --> 26:54.320
[continues chattering]

26:56.440 --> 26:57.760
[phone ringing]

27:00.880 --> 27:02.480
Small Heath 5-5-2.

27:03.080 --> 27:04.200
[Luca] Mrs. Gray.

27:05.560 --> 27:07.520
I wanted to illustrate to you

27:08.920 --> 27:10.080
that at any time

27:11.080 --> 27:12.440
we can reach your son.

27:16.040 --> 27:17.560
And we will kill your son...

27:18.800 --> 27:21.240
If you do not deliver Tommy Shelby

27:22.120 --> 27:23.480
as you promised. Huh?

27:29.000 --> 27:31.080
You just tell us where and when

27:31.880 --> 27:33.080
and we'll do the rest.

27:35.960 --> 27:37.240
[gasps]

27:51.000 --> 27:51.920
Where is he?

27:52.880 --> 27:53.880
Fucking.

27:54.000 --> 27:54.960
Fucking what?

27:55.560 --> 27:56.640
Fucking her.

28:01.760 --> 28:03.080
Ten thousand pounds?

28:03.320 --> 28:05.280
She left it blank
so I filled it in for her.

28:07.360 --> 28:09.760
What's Tommy's diary looking like
for the rest of the week?

28:09.840 --> 28:12.560
Oh, fuck Tommy and his cock and his gin.

28:13.640 --> 28:15.440
And his stuck-up whore.

28:17.440 --> 28:18.560
I saved this.

28:19.200 --> 28:20.360
Read my leaves, Pol?

28:21.320 --> 28:23.000
You haven't done my leaves in ages.

28:23.560 --> 28:25.120
Haven't done anyone's leaves for ages.

28:26.840 --> 28:27.800
Okay.

28:29.320 --> 28:30.240
Pour.

28:35.440 --> 28:36.480
Swirl the tea cup.

28:40.480 --> 28:41.760
Think about the man you love.

28:42.960 --> 28:43.840
Fuck him.

28:44.760 --> 28:46.360
Think about your white wedding gown.

28:48.680 --> 28:49.800
[exhales]

28:50.760 --> 28:52.280
Now pour the tea back into the pot.

28:54.680 --> 28:55.800
Okay?

28:56.480 --> 28:57.680
I'll read your tea leaves,

28:58.480 --> 28:59.960
if you go and get Tommy's diary.

29:14.520 --> 29:15.560
[door opens]

29:20.520 --> 29:23.280
See if Tommy's got
a free afternoon in the next seven days?

29:27.240 --> 29:28.280
Friday.

29:29.240 --> 29:31.960
Now read my future
and make it fucking good.

29:32.520 --> 29:34.520
Make sure he keeps it free.

29:35.520 --> 29:37.040
Just read my leaves, Pol.

29:37.440 --> 29:38.440
Already have.

29:39.920 --> 29:41.000
[blows air]

29:41.240 --> 29:42.240
Plain as day.

29:44.080 --> 29:45.200
What are you doing?

29:45.720 --> 29:48.320
Stop drinking whiskey. Switch to stout.

29:48.520 --> 29:49.400
Why?

29:52.840 --> 29:54.760
Make sure Tommy keeps that Friday free.

29:58.000 --> 29:59.560
Tell him I have an appointment for him.

30:00.000 --> 30:01.520
And then go and see a doctor

30:02.320 --> 30:04.280
about that baby in your tea leaves.

30:08.520 --> 30:10.560
[rock music playing]

30:40.680 --> 30:42.160
[groans]

30:44.880 --> 30:45.800
Next.

30:46.280 --> 30:48.600
You're not in the
fucking fairground now, son.

30:49.080 --> 30:50.760
Get down and skip me 200

30:50.880 --> 30:53.080
for taking the piss out of a fine fighter.

30:56.040 --> 30:58.600
-He's got a head on him that boy.
-[Aberama] And a fist.

30:59.960 --> 31:01.360
So, what do you say, Mr. May?

31:01.760 --> 31:03.520
He works hard, his game.

31:03.960 --> 31:05.680
I need to work on his defense.

31:06.360 --> 31:08.760
Right now his left hand
is just good for wanking.

31:09.520 --> 31:12.400
You don't have to earn your money,
we're paying you anyway, is he ready?

31:13.400 --> 31:14.600
Is he even registered?

31:17.560 --> 31:19.240
I've put him down as a welterweight.

31:19.320 --> 31:20.480
Midlands division.

31:20.640 --> 31:23.520
Amateur turning pro.
Just need your counter signature.

31:26.040 --> 31:27.960
Like I'm going to say no to you boys.

31:29.040 --> 31:30.320
Just tell us.

31:30.720 --> 31:31.680
Is he ready?

31:34.560 --> 31:36.040
[Mr. May] In 30 years,

31:36.640 --> 31:38.720
I've never seen a war boy so ready.

31:39.120 --> 31:40.520
[Bonnie exhales]

31:41.040 --> 31:42.520
And that's 200, Mr. May.

31:44.160 --> 31:45.760
So, who am I fighting first, Mr. Shelby?

31:48.240 --> 31:49.560
When you step in the ring, Bonnie,

31:50.120 --> 31:51.880
who do you see across the canvas, eh?

31:52.800 --> 31:53.760
I see myself.

31:55.200 --> 31:58.200
Forty years old having done fuck all
except tramping the lines.

31:59.240 --> 32:00.440
See my life wasted.

32:01.440 --> 32:02.920
I won't let it happen.

32:03.680 --> 32:05.080
Me? I'm going to be a champion.

32:06.880 --> 32:08.000
[speaking Romani]

32:08.080 --> 32:09.760
When this business is done...

32:10.880 --> 32:14.200
You'll be champion of the world Gypsy boy.

32:15.760 --> 32:17.280
[in English] First heard in Camden Town.

32:18.200 --> 32:20.440
Boy's name is Goliath.

32:24.520 --> 32:26.800
[peppy music playing]

32:43.000 --> 32:44.160
Said he's at a meeting.

32:44.960 --> 32:48.000
Yes. I was wondering
do you have any boots that I can borrow?

32:48.320 --> 32:49.440
Why do you need boots?

32:50.080 --> 32:51.680
Well, no one here will speak to me.

32:51.760 --> 32:53.640
So, I thought I would
take a walk by the canal.

32:56.000 --> 32:57.560
Is he really making gin?

32:58.000 --> 33:00.080
He's going mad
cooped up here is what he is.

33:00.520 --> 33:02.280
Like a wasp in a beer glass.

33:05.440 --> 33:06.520
Um...

33:07.400 --> 33:09.040
You'll look a bit funny in these I think.

33:09.160 --> 33:10.400
[chuckles]

33:10.920 --> 33:12.240
Thank you, Curly.

33:16.200 --> 33:19.040
If you're going to take a walk along
the canal, you'll also need this.

33:20.160 --> 33:21.320
Are you serious?

33:22.520 --> 33:24.480
Italians north. Italians south.

33:25.600 --> 33:28.560
He makes gin to take his mind off
being stuck inside a prison.

33:28.880 --> 33:30.080
[gun clicking]

33:30.840 --> 33:32.280
If you had any sense, Mrs. Carleton,

33:32.600 --> 33:33.920
you would get out of here now.

33:34.720 --> 33:37.800
Curly can sail you to Snowhill Station
hidden in the coal sacks.

33:40.520 --> 33:41.520
I said I would wait.

33:42.120 --> 33:44.200
The man you are waiting for doesn't exist.

33:46.240 --> 33:47.440
[Curly stammers]

33:47.520 --> 33:49.880
While you're waiting
for the man who doesn't exist,

33:49.960 --> 33:51.560
would you like to try some of his gin?

33:55.080 --> 33:56.880
[music continues]

34:06.360 --> 34:08.120
[Devlin] The strike has blocked
the Coventry Road.

34:09.360 --> 34:10.800
Made all the men get off the buses.

34:13.280 --> 34:14.280
Where were the police?

34:15.720 --> 34:16.720
They were busy.

34:18.600 --> 34:21.480
Apparently there was a report of
armed men in the Small Heath area.

34:22.680 --> 34:24.560
And there was a murder

34:25.000 --> 34:26.400
at the Summer Lane Hospital.

34:27.200 --> 34:29.120
And something happening
on the Stratford Road.

34:31.680 --> 34:33.680
The strike has
threatened the men's families.

34:34.560 --> 34:37.320
They said come the revolution,
their names would be on the list.

34:38.240 --> 34:39.120
Like in Russia.

34:41.320 --> 34:42.480
And the buses turned round?

34:42.960 --> 34:44.960
Yes. They turned round.

35:06.160 --> 35:07.000
Mr. Shelby.

35:09.320 --> 35:11.800
Factories are shut. Mines are shut.

35:12.280 --> 35:13.920
Coal's running out.

35:16.280 --> 35:18.360
Did you ever consider the possibility

35:18.680 --> 35:20.000
that the Communists might win?

35:21.200 --> 35:22.320
And you and me,

35:22.600 --> 35:24.160
traitors to our class,

35:24.240 --> 35:26.600
will be put up against a wall and shot?

35:28.360 --> 35:29.400
As a businessman,

35:30.840 --> 35:32.560
I consider all possibilities.

35:35.520 --> 35:36.720
But Mr. Devlin,

35:38.720 --> 35:40.200
I'm not a traitor to my class.

35:43.280 --> 35:45.200
Small Heath 5-5-1.

35:50.760 --> 35:52.520
I'm just an extreme example

35:53.680 --> 35:55.240
of what a working man can achieve.

36:01.240 --> 36:02.280
Hello, Ada.

36:02.680 --> 36:03.640
Yeah, it's me.

36:05.560 --> 36:06.400
I need your help.

36:07.280 --> 36:09.040
[May singing]

36:11.080 --> 36:12.400
[Tommy] Four o'clock.

36:14.280 --> 36:15.120
[May] Is it?

36:16.080 --> 36:17.160
What do you think?

36:17.560 --> 36:19.840
Oh, nice. A little sweet.

36:22.600 --> 36:23.880
I've heard not sweet enough.

36:25.680 --> 36:26.640
Come and see.

36:30.280 --> 36:31.480
[door opens]

36:36.960 --> 36:38.480
[May] My God, Tommy.

36:40.120 --> 36:41.360
What is this, a hobby?

36:42.280 --> 36:44.560
No. Don't really have time for hobbies.

36:46.120 --> 36:47.440
This is more of, uh...

36:49.520 --> 36:51.200
More of a place where I try things out.

36:53.640 --> 36:56.560
I decided if I'm going to be
stuck here in Small Heath,

36:57.560 --> 36:58.960
might as well do something useful.

37:00.680 --> 37:01.760
Less of a hobby,

37:02.320 --> 37:03.560
more of a venture.

37:10.200 --> 37:12.440
Since the start of
prohibition I've been sending

37:12.640 --> 37:15.280
single malt, scotch whiskey

37:16.000 --> 37:18.600
to Boston and Halifax, Nova Scotia

37:19.520 --> 37:21.000
hidden in crates of car parts.

37:21.120 --> 37:22.040
[chuckles]

37:22.840 --> 37:25.520
But my supply lines
from Scotland have been unreliable.

37:26.400 --> 37:27.960
There have been a lot of thefts.

37:28.160 --> 37:29.360
And in this modern age,

37:29.880 --> 37:32.360
American women drink
as much booze as the men.

37:33.000 --> 37:36.240
And women apparently prefer gin.

37:36.440 --> 37:37.560
[pours drink]

37:38.560 --> 37:40.800
So with the help
of a friend of mine in Camden Town,

37:41.360 --> 37:43.120
I set up my own source of supply.

37:44.440 --> 37:46.280
Junipers, potatoes,

37:47.440 --> 37:48.640
sugar and water,

37:49.400 --> 37:51.120
all turned into U.S. dollars.

37:54.320 --> 37:55.440
When we're up and running,

37:55.680 --> 37:58.080
we'll produce over 200 gallons a week.

38:01.200 --> 38:02.360
But before then...

38:04.320 --> 38:05.880
I need to get my recipe right.

38:10.680 --> 38:11.680
So tell me the truth?

38:25.960 --> 38:27.600
-The truth?
-Mmm-hmm.

38:29.880 --> 38:31.600
You're unlike any man I have ever met.

38:34.480 --> 38:35.360
And the gin?

38:36.200 --> 38:37.040
Too sweet.

38:57.840 --> 38:59.000
You said you booked a room?

39:00.280 --> 39:01.480
No, I booked a suite.

39:04.520 --> 39:05.560
Thank you.

39:07.720 --> 39:09.320
If you book a taxi, I'll go.

39:13.120 --> 39:14.720
You're a businessman, Tommy, a good one.

39:16.440 --> 39:18.440
-If only you could...
-If only I could what?

39:21.120 --> 39:22.440
"If only you could change."

39:22.960 --> 39:25.960
Go on, say it.
If only you could change the bad.

39:27.560 --> 39:29.840
And the good is laying off
a thousand men, which I do

39:30.120 --> 39:31.440
like a good businessman.

39:32.240 --> 39:33.440
And I do it like that.
[snaps fingers]

39:34.720 --> 39:35.960
And people go hungry.

39:36.520 --> 39:37.720
And the bad,

39:38.240 --> 39:40.320
the bad is fucking winning the horses

39:40.720 --> 39:41.600
and a gun

39:42.400 --> 39:44.040
and some fucking self-respect.

39:45.680 --> 39:47.000
You fucking people.

39:52.600 --> 39:53.880
You bite like your horse.

39:59.400 --> 40:00.960
I don't trust taxis anymore.

40:02.400 --> 40:04.000
Come with me.

40:13.720 --> 40:14.800
[Arthur] There you go, brother.

40:16.280 --> 40:18.320
Home sweet home.

40:19.120 --> 40:21.000
Place for you to bring women back,

40:21.320 --> 40:22.840
bring your mates,

40:23.200 --> 40:26.320
play games, gamble, cards and

40:27.040 --> 40:28.680
you keep an eye on our back yard.

40:29.160 --> 40:30.240
[glass clatters]

40:30.440 --> 40:33.400
Artillery Square.
Place is full of Communists.

40:34.040 --> 40:35.040
The strikers.

40:35.760 --> 40:38.120
Having a Shelby round here
will keep them in order.

40:40.520 --> 40:42.000
Light the fucking fire.

40:42.400 --> 40:44.080
Take some of the shit off the walls.

40:44.560 --> 40:47.920
Curly will be over later
with some poison for the rats.

40:48.200 --> 40:49.240
-[Finn] Arthur?
-Huh?

40:50.840 --> 40:51.680
This morning...

40:53.760 --> 40:55.760
I wouldn't have
been able to pull that trigger.

40:56.280 --> 40:59.320
I know I wouldn't.
They would have just walked past.

41:01.160 --> 41:02.120
I'm not John.

41:03.760 --> 41:05.080
-I know I'm not.
-All right.

41:08.080 --> 41:09.360
You're my brother.

41:11.000 --> 41:12.080
I know you're not.

41:12.920 --> 41:13.880
All right?

41:15.680 --> 41:16.640
In the end,

41:17.800 --> 41:19.640
it's God who pulls
that fucking trigger anyway.

41:21.040 --> 41:25.080
We don't get to decide
who lives and who dies, Finn.

41:26.320 --> 41:27.400
Not us.

41:30.560 --> 41:31.800
You just have to...

41:33.000 --> 41:33.920
Flick a switch...

41:34.800 --> 41:35.760
In your head.

41:54.920 --> 41:57.120
Charlie said it wasn't safe
to walk by the canal.

41:57.880 --> 41:58.840
He's right.

42:00.320 --> 42:02.520
[May] Italians north and south, he said.

42:03.480 --> 42:04.440
[Tommy] No.

42:04.840 --> 42:07.200
It's mostly chancers, local kids,

42:08.080 --> 42:09.360
trying to get famous.

42:09.520 --> 42:10.840
Sabini's boys.

42:11.640 --> 42:14.160
Sheffield boys, Manchester, Glasgow.

42:14.760 --> 42:16.360
They all know we're on the ropes.

42:17.280 --> 42:19.040
And that's not including the mafia.

42:19.200 --> 42:23.600
Yep. Here's your carriage.
You can catch the 7:15 train.

42:24.280 --> 42:25.600
I thought there was a strike?

42:26.520 --> 42:27.720
Who gave you that idea?

42:29.280 --> 42:30.320
My God!

42:32.000 --> 42:34.480
I know. The bad I can't help.

42:36.120 --> 42:37.400
I wanted you to stay.

42:38.400 --> 42:39.920
And normally I get my own way.

42:41.840 --> 42:43.760
Losing occasionally makes me worse.

42:53.880 --> 42:55.280
Thank you, Curly.

42:57.160 --> 42:59.320
She looks a picture in my boots,
doesn't she, Tom?

42:59.760 --> 43:01.560
Walk her to the train, Curly.

43:01.840 --> 43:02.800
Yeah.

43:03.720 --> 43:05.320
[woman] Not a minute on the day.

43:05.880 --> 43:07.640
Not a penny of the pay.

43:08.160 --> 43:10.160
This talk of national austerity,

43:10.280 --> 43:14.920
this talk of tightening our belts
must be met with resolute certainty.

43:15.480 --> 43:17.720
We will not accept any cuts in pay.

43:17.960 --> 43:21.760
We will not accept working
longer hours for the same wage.

43:22.520 --> 43:25.040
Shoulder to shoulder with the coal miners,

43:25.240 --> 43:28.120
dock workers,
railway men and steel workers.

43:28.560 --> 43:29.920
We will stand united

43:30.160 --> 43:31.280
through the storm

43:31.400 --> 43:33.600
to show they come into our city.

43:33.880 --> 43:35.840
[workers cheering]

43:47.160 --> 43:50.200
Goodnight. See you soon. Take care.

43:50.800 --> 43:52.040
Jessie Eden?

43:52.320 --> 43:53.480
Yes?

43:53.920 --> 43:55.440
I'd like to buy you a drink.

43:56.000 --> 43:59.160
No. I'm afraid the pubs around here don't
allow unaccompanied female drinkers.

43:59.560 --> 44:00.920
I'm sure we'll be fine.

44:02.080 --> 44:03.400
I know you from somewhere.

44:04.120 --> 44:05.240
I'm Ada Thorn,

44:06.440 --> 44:07.560
formerly Shelby.

44:09.560 --> 44:10.800
I have a settlement proposition

44:10.880 --> 44:14.480
that my brother's too arrogant
and proud to offer you in person.

44:17.880 --> 44:19.440
[indistinct chatter]

44:24.160 --> 44:25.760
[chattering stops]

44:26.480 --> 44:27.640
Sorry, ladies, we don't...

44:31.120 --> 44:32.080
Don't what?

44:33.640 --> 44:35.680
It's all right. I'm sorry, I didn't...

44:35.880 --> 44:37.160
"What are you drinking ladies?"

44:37.560 --> 44:39.000
That's what you're supposed to say.

44:39.120 --> 44:41.760
Yes. Sorry, uh, what are you drinking?

44:42.280 --> 44:43.480
Beer. Pint.

44:44.000 --> 44:45.120
And a whiskey on ice.

44:45.920 --> 44:47.120
I'm afraid we don't have ice.

44:48.480 --> 44:50.320
Next time make sure you have ice.

44:50.920 --> 44:53.440
This pub's come
to our attention for its lack of ice.

44:55.600 --> 44:57.360
[men chattering]

45:06.160 --> 45:08.200
[Ada] There is more than one way
to achieve equality.

45:12.080 --> 45:14.040
Before you begin to congratulate yourself,

45:14.800 --> 45:17.080
I'm not impressed with your
power to terrify people.

45:17.240 --> 45:18.400
Hmm.

45:18.760 --> 45:20.880
Whereas, I am impressed by your ability

45:20.960 --> 45:23.120
to stand up in front
of an audience and speak.

45:23.800 --> 45:25.080
I was never able to do it.

45:25.480 --> 45:27.040
I've heard people talk about you.

45:28.000 --> 45:29.800
You used to advocate armed revolution.

45:29.880 --> 45:33.880
Shelby Company Limited is prepared to
offer parity for male and female workers

45:34.360 --> 45:36.320
across all the factories in our ownership

45:36.640 --> 45:38.080
and withdraw the pay cut for men.

45:38.480 --> 45:39.640
In return for what?

45:45.360 --> 45:47.280
In return for something very unusual

45:47.920 --> 45:49.600
but very typical of my brother.

45:49.880 --> 45:51.000
What does he want?

45:51.840 --> 45:53.080
To sit down with you and talk.

45:54.400 --> 45:56.400
If he withdraws his pay cut
we've no need to talk.

45:57.560 --> 46:00.200
He wants to talk about
socialism and revolution.

46:01.680 --> 46:03.400
Dear God, are you serious?

46:04.040 --> 46:05.200
My brother's a book maker,

46:05.800 --> 46:07.360
book makers hedge their bets.

46:07.560 --> 46:09.160
Sometimes long shots come in.

46:09.840 --> 46:11.400
And because we emptied his buses today,

46:11.480 --> 46:13.280
he thinks we are amongst
the runners and riders?

46:14.160 --> 46:16.400
Beneath it all,
my brother's a very rational man.

46:16.560 --> 46:17.480
Beneath all what?

46:18.120 --> 46:20.520
You mean beneath the beatings,
the cuttings, the shootings,

46:20.640 --> 46:22.200
-the murders.
-Yeah, beneath all that.

46:25.240 --> 46:26.480
Tell your brother that

46:26.800 --> 46:30.120
when we take power all means of
production will be owned by the workers.

46:30.400 --> 46:33.200
Essential industries will
be taken under state control.

46:33.280 --> 46:34.400
Birmingham Town Hall

46:34.480 --> 46:36.800
is a rather beautiful building
and we plan to preserve it.

46:37.400 --> 46:38.800
What else does he need to know?

46:42.560 --> 46:43.560
It's going to happen.

46:46.560 --> 46:48.600
Aren't you a bit sorry
you jumped ship too soon?

46:50.560 --> 46:52.000
He wants to take you for dinner.

46:53.320 --> 46:54.840
I really have nothing to wear.

46:55.160 --> 46:56.400
You'd need to wear boots,

46:57.160 --> 46:59.520
